artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a classical many-body Hamiltonian system with phase space $\Gamma$, equipped with the Liouville measure, and a Hamiltonian $H$ generating reversible microscopic dynamics $T_t:\Gamma	o\Gamma$. A statistical state at time $t$ is described by a probability density $ho_t$ on $\Gamma$, evolving via the Liouville equation $\partial_tho_t = \{H,ho_t\}$. Suppose that, prior to time $t=0$, an experimenter performs an external preparation procedure on the system, modeled as a (possibly time-dependent) modification of the Hamiltonian and/or contact with auxiliary systems, after which the system is released to evolve under $H$ alone.

Call an initial ensemble $ho_0$ \emph{relaxation-admissible} if it satisfies the following:
(i) it is supported on a sufficiently large region of $\Gamma$ consistent with the prescribed macroscopic constraints (e.g. energy, particle number);
(ii) it has a comparatively simple functional form, e.g. expressible through a small number of macroscopic Lagrange parameters via a maximum-entropy prescription;
(iii) under the dynamics $T_t$, the macroscopic observables $\{Q_j(t)=\int Q_j\,ho_t\,d\Gamma\}$ exhibit irreversible, asymmetric relaxation toward equilibrium values;
(iv) the relaxation times associated with $\{Q_j\}$ are finite.

Problem. Give a rigorous, constructive characterization of the class of external preparation protocols (acting on the microscopic dynamics in a finite time interval prior to $t=0$) that produce, from an arbitrary pre-preparation state, an initial ensemble $ho_0$ which is relaxation-admissible in the sense above. In particular, identify necessary and sufficient conditions on such preparation protocols ensuring that the resulting $ho_0$ (a) lies in the appropriate maximum-entropy manifold, (b) yields strictly positive finite relaxation times for a designated set of macroscopic observables, and (c) excludes the time-reversed ensembles that would produce anti-thermodynamic behavior.
